                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Which RoC handles partnership to Pvt Ltd conversion in Delhi?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>The RoC, NCT of Delhi and Haryana, at 4th Floor, IFCI Tower, 61 Nehru Place, New Delhi - 110019 handles all conversion filings under Section 366. All filings (URC-1, SPICe+) are electronic through the MCA portal. Patron's Delhi team files regularly with this office.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the fee for partnership to Pvt Ltd conversion in Delhi?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>From Rs 14,999 with Patron (all-inclusive). Government fees include Rs 1,000 for RUN, SPICe+ (free for capital up to Rs 15 lakh), Delhi stamp duty (nominal) and newspaper costs (Rs 2,000-5,000). Delhi's low stamp duty and zero Professional Tax make it more affordable than Maharashtra or Karnataka.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">How long does partnership to Pvt Ltd conversion take in Delhi?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>30-45 working days. The biggest component is the mandatory 21-day newspaper objection period under Section 366. Pre-conversion steps take 5-10 days. RoC Delhi processes URC-1 in 7-15 working days after the objection period. Patron's pre-screening minimises rejection risk.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Is capital gains tax payable on conversion?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>No capital gains if S.47(xiii) conditions are met: all assets/liabilities transfer, all partners become shareholders in same proportion, partners retain 50%+ voting power for 5 years, no asset revaluation in preceding 3 years. Breach triggers clawback under S.47A(3). Patron's CA team structures all conditions correctly.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can an unregistered firm convert to Pvt Ltd in Delhi?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Section 366 allows both registered and unregistered firms to apply. However, unregistered firms must submit additional documents proving existence. Registration with Registrar of Firms, Delhi is recommended for faster RoC approval.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What happens to existing contracts after conversion?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Under S.366(4), all contracts, employment agreements, rights and obligations automatically vest in the new company. Licences and permits (Delhi Shop Act, FSSAI, trade licence, GST) must be separately updated with issuing authorities.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-7">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the newspaper advertisement requirement?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Mandatory URC-2 publication in one English and one Hindi newspaper circulating in Delhi, followed by 21-day objection period. This step is unique to Section 366 conversions and cannot be skipped. Patron handles ad placement through Delhi publications.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>


